Bayt Al Hekma )الحكمة بيت )Library Management
System
Complete Program Description
A full account of what the program is, what it models, how every part behaves, and why
it is built the way it is.

1. What This Program Is
The Bayt Al Hekma Library Management System is a console application that replaces a
community librarian’s wooden card index.
It does two jobs. The first is circulation: keeping track of a catalogue of borrowable
items, who currently holds each one, what state each item is in, and how long each may
be kept. The second is fines: working out what a late return costs, applying the correct
waiver for the member’s category, adding an administrative charge that is deliberately
not waivable, and recording the debt against the member so it can be paid off later.
The program is built around a single idea: the library holds items that are similar
enough to be handled together, but different enough that each must be allowed to
answer for itself. Every item can state how long it may be kept and what it costs to
return late, but no two item types answer either question the same way. The program is
arranged so that the code which loops over the catalogue never needs to know which
specific kind of item it is holding.
Everything else in this document follows from that one idea.
2. The Building Blocks
This section describes every class: what it is responsible for, what it holds, what it can
do, and what it deliberately refuses to do.
2.4 Membership categories
Three categories exist, each carrying a fixed waiver rate applied to overdue fines:
Category Waiver
Student 25% of the fine waived
Staff 10% waived
Public none

A category is fixed at registration and never changes. The waiver applies to the fine itself
and never to the administrative charge.
2.5 Fines
A fine is not an object in this program — it is a calculation performed at the moment of
return, producing a number that is immediately charged to a member and then exists
only as part of that member’s balance.
This is a deliberate simplification and a real limitation. The library can tell you what a
member owes in total, but not what each individual fine was for. Section 10 returns to
this.
3.1 ItemStatus — an enumeration
Four values: AVAILABLE, ON_LOAN, RESERVED, LOST.
An item is always in exactly one of these states and there is no fifth possibility. Using a
fixed set of values rather than free text means an invalid state is impossible to write
down — a misspelling becomes a compile error rather than a bug discovered at the
desk.
3.2 MembershipType — an enumeration that carries data
Three values, each holding its own waiver rate. Unlike ItemStatus, this is not merely a
set of labels: each value is an object that knows its own rate and can be asked for it.
The consequence is that the rest of the program contains no table mapping category
names to percentages. The category is the rate. Adding a fourth category is a one-line
change in one file.
3.3 Renewable — an interface
Two capabilities:
• Renew the loan — attempt to extend it, and report whether it worked
• Report the renewal limit — how many times this item may be renewed in total
This is a contract about what an object can do, not about what an object is. Books and
magazines sign this contract; DVDs do not.
The distinction matters. All three types are library items — they share an identity. Only
two of them can be renewed — they share a capability. Modelling the first with
inheritance and the second with an interface is what allows a DVD to be a perfectly
ordinary catalogue item in every other respect while remaining impossible to renew.
Had renewability been modelled as a kind of item instead, the hierarchy would have had
to split before it began, and every shared behaviour would have been duplicated on
both sides of the split.

3.4 Member
Holds: a name, a membership ID that never changes, a category that never changes, a
balance owed, and a count of items currently held.
Can: report all of these; have its name corrected; be charged a fine; accept a payment;
report whether it is currently allowed to borrow; record a borrowing; record a return.
Refuses: to let anyone set the balance or the item count directly. There are no such
operations. Both numbers move only through controlled paths:
• Charging a fine rejects any amount that is not positive, then adds it.
• Paying a fine rejects any amount that is not positive, rejects any amount larger
than the balance owed — a member cannot overpay — and only then deducts it.
It reports back whether it succeeded.
• Recording a borrowing increases the count by one.
• Recording a return decreases the count by one, and refuses to go below zero.
The borrowing eligibility check is worth noting separately. A member may borrow only
if they hold fewer than three items and owe no more than 100 EGP. The member
answers this question itself rather than having the library inspect its fields and decide.
The rule about who may borrow lives with the thing the rule is about.
Two constructors exist: a new member starting from nothing, and a member carried
over from the old card index who already owes money. The first defers to the second
rather than repeating the setup, so there is exactly one place where a member is
actually assembled.
3.5 LibraryItem — an abstract class
Holds, shared across every item type: a permanent catalogue ID, a permanent title, a
current state, a borrower name, and a renewal count. It also holds three facts that
belong to the library rather than to any individual item: the library’s name, the
administrative charge, and a running count of how many items have ever been
catalogued.
Can:
• Report all of its properties
• Be marked reserved or lost, and brought back
• Lend itself to a member — but only if it is currently available. If it is on loan,
reserved, or lost, the attempt fails and says so.
• Take itself back — reset the state, clear the borrower, reset the renewal count
• Record a renewal against the current loan
• Display itself as one line: ID, category, title, state, borrower, loan period, and the
fine for being one day overdue

Cannot be created directly. LibraryItem is abstract. Asking for “just a library item” is a
question with no answer — there is no rule for how long a generic item may be kept or
what it costs to return late — so the program refuses to let anyone ask.
Demands of every item type three answers the parent cannot supply:
• What does it cost to return this item a given number of days late?
• How many days may this type be kept?
• What is this category called?
The parent knows every item has an answer to all three. It has no idea what any of them
are.
Two design commitments worth stating explicitly:
The display method is written once. All three item types display identically, and that
shared method calls all three of the demanded methods — none of which the parent
implements. This works because at the moment the display runs, the object is never a
bare LibraryItem; it is always some specific kind, and that kind’s answers are the ones
that execute.
Taking an item back cannot be overridden. An item type is free to invent its own fine
rule and its own loan period. It is not free to redefine what returning means. If a type
could override the return behaviour and forget to reset the state, that item would
remain permanently on loan — and nothing would report an error. It would simply
vanish from the library’s available stock, quietly, forever. Preventing that is not a

security measure; it is the original designer stating that this particular behaviour is load-
bearing.

3.6 The three item types
Each extends LibraryItem, passes the ID and title up to the parent, adds the extra
fields it needs, and answers the three demanded questions.
Book. Adds an author and a page count. May be kept 14 days. Costs 5.00 EGP per
overdue day, without limit. Renewable up to twice.
Magazine. Adds an issue number. May be kept 7 days. Costs 3.00 EGP per overdue day,
but the total stops at 30.00 EGP however long it is kept. Renewable once.
DVD. Adds a runtime. May be kept 3 days. Costs 15.00 EGP per overdue day, without
limit. Not renewable.
The magazine’s cap is the single most instructive line in the program. Where the book
and the DVD differ from each other only by a number, the magazine differs by shape —
its rule contains a maximum and therefore cannot be expressed as “the daily rate times
the days” at all. A design that centralised the fine calculation and parameterised the rate

would have to grow a special case here. The design used instead does not notice,
because it never assumed the three rules were related in the first place.
3.7 Library
The library holds two collections — the catalogue and the member register — each with
a count of how many entries are actually in use.
Registering things. An item is refused if the catalogue is full or its ID is already taken. A
member is refused if the register is full or the membership ID is already taken.
Finding things. Two searches: an item by catalogue ID, and a member by membership
ID. Both return nothing rather than failing when there is no match, and every caller is
expected to handle that.
Listing things. Three listings: the whole catalogue; items filtered by state; and all
members with what each one owes.
Calculating things. Four figures:
• Items on loan — how many are currently out
• Loan rate — items on loan as a percentage of the catalogue
• Total outstanding — every member’s balance, added up
• Projected fines — a budgeting figure the head librarian asks for monthly: if every
item currently on loan came back a given number of days late, what would the
library be owed before waivers?
That last figure is where the polymorphism becomes visible as a number rather than a
listing. It asks every item on loan the same question, and a book, a magazine, and a DVD
each answer differently — with the magazine’s answer capped. One loop, three rules,
no type checking.
Operations. Three composite actions:
Lending. Find the item and the member; refuse if either is missing, if the item is not
available, or if the member is not eligible. On success, record the borrower on the item
and increase the member’s held count.
Processing a return. Find the item; refuse if it is not on loan or the overdue count is
negative. Find the member from the item’s recorded borrower name — and this must
happen before the item is taken back, because taking it back clears that name. Then
calculate the fine, charge it, decrease the member’s held count, print the breakdown,
and free the item.
Attempting a renewal. Find the item and report if it does not exist. Then ask whether
the item has the renewal capability at all. If it does, attempt the renewal — which can
still fail if the item is not on loan or has already reached its limit. If it does not, explain
that this item type cannot be renewed.

This capability check is the single place in the entire program where an object’s type is
examined at runtime. It is permitted because of what it asks. It does not ask “is this a
book or a magazine?” — it asks “can this object be renewed?” The first question would
mean the design had failed; the second is the entire reason interfaces exist.
Reporting. One report covering both halves of the system: catalogue size, items ever
catalogued, items on loan, the loan rate, total outstanding fines across all members, and
the projected fine figure.
3.8 Main
Builds the library, populates it with items and members, then loops: print the menu,
read a choice, dispatch it, repeat until the user exits.
One detail is worth pointing at. Three different kinds of item are created and all of them
are handed to the same collection. From that moment on, the library holds a set of
items and has no record of which specific kind each one was — and never needs one.

7. Every Operation In Detail
The program presents ten operations plus an exit.
1 — View catalogue. No input. Loops over every item and prints one line each: ID,
category, title, state, borrower, loan period, and one-day fine. Three item types produce
three different loan periods and three different fines from one loop.
2 — Register member. Asks for a name, membership ID, and category. Refuses a
duplicate ID or a full register. Creates the member with a zero balance.
3 — Borrow item. Asks for an item ID and a membership ID. Refuses if either is missing,
if the item is not available, or if the member holds three items or owes more than 100
EGP. On success, records the borrower and confirms the loan period.
4 — Return item. Asks for an item ID and how many days overdue, where zero means
on time. Refuses if the item is not on loan or the count is negative. Calculates the fine in
the fixed order, charges it, prints an itemised breakdown, reports the member’s new
balance, and frees the item.
5 — Renew loan. Asks for an item ID. Checks first whether the type can be renewed at
all, explaining if not. If it can, attempts the renewal, which may still fail if the item is not
on loan or has hit its limit. Reports how many renewals remain.
6 — Search item by ID. Prints that one item’s details, or reports it was not found. Never
crashes.
7 — View items by status. Asks which state, then lists only items in it, or reports there
are none.

8 — Pay outstanding fines. Asks for a membership ID and an amount. Refuses a non-
positive amount and refuses an overpayment. Reports the new balance.

9 — View all members. Lists every member with name, ID, category, items held, and
balance owed.
10 — Library report. Catalogue size, items ever catalogued, items on loan, loan rate,
total outstanding across all members, and the projected fine figure for a five-day
overdue scenario.
0 — Exit. Ends the loop.
8. Every Rule The Program Enforces
Catalogue rules
1. Catalogue IDs are unique; a duplicate is refused at registration
2. A catalogue ID and title never change once the item is created
3. Lending is only possible from the available state
4. An item marked reserved or lost cannot be lent
5. Returning is only possible from the on-loan state
6. Returning always resets the state, borrower, and renewal count — and no item
type may redefine this
Member rules
7. Membership IDs are unique; a duplicate is refused at registration
8. A membership ID and category never change
9. A member may hold at most three items at once
10. A member owing more than 100.00 EGP cannot borrow
11. A fine charged must be positive
12. A payment must be positive and must not exceed the balance owed
13. The balance can never become negative
14. The count of items held can never become negative
15. Neither the balance nor the count can be set directly by any means
Fine rules
16. Days overdue must not be negative
17. Zero days overdue means nothing is charged, including no administrative charge
18. The magazine fine never exceeds 30.00 EGP however long the item is kept
19. The waiver applies to the base fine only, never to the administrative charge
20. The member must be identified from the item before the item is freed

Renewal rules
21. Only item types carrying the renewal capability can be renewed
22. An item can only be renewed while it is on loan
23. An item cannot be renewed beyond its type’s limit
24. Attempting to renew a type that cannot be renewed produces an explanation,
not a crash and not silence
General rules
25. Neither collection may exceed its capacity
26. Searching for something that does not exist reports it and never crashes
27. Every operation returns to the menu rather than terminating the program
